 "derivation": "English basket cannot be derived from Latin fascis. Klein, on the very page Nourai cites, writes: 'basket, n. - ME., fr. L. bascauda, \"a brazen vessel\", referred to by the Roman poet Martial as an OBrit. word; cogn. with L. fascis, \"bundle\"; see fasces' - i.e. bascauda is a cognate (a Brittonic word within the *bhasko- family), not a descendant of fascis. Etymonline is more sceptical still: basket (from Anglo-French bascat) is 'of obscure origin despite much speculation', and the OED doubts the bascauda connection because 'there is no evidence of such a word in Celtic'. Either way the drawn arrow Latin fascis -> English basket is wrong in form and in direction.",
